{"question":"What is Node.js?","answer":"A JavaScript runtime built on Chrome's V8 engine that lets you run JS outside the browser, mainly for building servers.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"Is Node.js single-threaded or multi-threaded?","answer":"Single-threaded for JS execution, but it uses libuv under the hood to offload heavy I/O work to a thread pool, so it can still handle many operations concurrently.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is the event loop?","answer":"A mechanism that lets Node handle async operations by continuously checking a queue of callbacks and executing them once the current stack is clear, without blocking the main thread.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between synchronous and asynchronous code in Node?","answer":"Synchronous code blocks execution until it finishes; asynchronous code lets Node move on to other tasks and comes back via a callback, promise, or async/await once it's done.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is a callback function?","answer":"A function passed as an argument to another function, to be executed later once some operation completes.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is \"callback hell\"?","answer":"Deeply nested callbacks that become hard to read and maintain, usually solved with Promises or async/await.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is a Promise?","answer":"An object representing the eventual result of an async operation, with three states: pending, fulfilled, or rejected.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What does async/await do?","answer":"It's syntax sugar over Promises that lets you write async code that looks synchronous, using try/catch for error handling instead of .then/.catch chains.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between require and import?","answer":"require is CommonJS, synchronous, used natively in Node. import is ES Modules syntax, and needs \"type\": \"module\" in package.json or a transpiler to work in Node.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is module.exports?","answer":"An object that determines what a file exposes when required elsewhere in the app.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is npm?","answer":"Node's package manager — used to install, manage, and publish JavaScript packages.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between dependencies and devDependencies in package.json?","answer":"dependencies are needed to run the app in production; devDependencies are only needed during development (like testing tools or linters).","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is package-lock.json for?","answer":"It locks the exact versions of installed packages (and their sub-dependencies) so installs are consistent across machines.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is the fs module used for?","answer":"Reading, writing, and manipulating files on the filesystem.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is the difference between fs.readFile and fs.readFileSync?","answer":"readFile is asynchronous and doesn't block the event loop; readFileSync is synchronous and blocks execution until the file is read.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is the purpose of the path module?","answer":"It helps handle and manipulate file paths in a way that works across different operating systems.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is an environment variable and why use them?","answer":"A variable set outside your code (often via .env) used to store configuration like API keys or database URLs, keeping secrets out of your codebase.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What does process.env do?","answer":"Gives access to environment variables inside a Node app.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is middleware in the context of Node servers generally?","answer":"A function that runs between receiving a request and sending a response, often used for logging, auth checks, or modifying the request/response.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between process.nextTick() and setImmediate()?","answer":"process.nextTick() runs before the event loop continues to the next phase; setImmediate() runs on the next iteration of the event loop, after I/O events.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is a memory leak in Node.js?","answer":"When memory that's no longer needed isn't released, often caused by things like uncleared intervals, global variables holding references, or unclosed connections.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is clustering in Node.js?","answer":"Using the cluster module to spawn multiple Node processes that share the same server port, taking advantage of multi-core CPUs since Node itself is single-threaded.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is the difference between a global object and a module-level scope in Node?","answer":"Global objects (like process) are accessible everywhere; module-level variables are scoped to that file unless exported.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's an EventEmitter?","answer":"A Node.js core class that lets objects emit named events and other parts of the code listen and react to them — the pattern Socket.io itself is built on.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What does npm run dev typically do?","answer":"Executes the \"dev\" script defined in package.json, commonly used to start a dev server with tools like nodemon for auto-restart.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is nodemon?","answer":"A tool that automatically restarts your Node server whenever it detects file changes, useful during development.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between a blocking and non-blocking operation?","answer":"A blocking operation halts further code execution until it finishes; non-blocking lets other code run while it completes in the background.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is the purpose of the os module?","answer":"Provides information about the operating system, like CPU architecture, memory, and platform.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What happens if an uncaught exception occurs in Node?","answer":"By default, it crashes the process — which is why global error handlers and proper try/catch are important in production apps.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between exports and module.exports?","answer":"exports is a reference to module.exports; if you reassign exports directly (exports = something), it breaks the reference, so it's safer to always use module.exports.","topic":"Node.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is Express.js?","answer":"A minimal, unopinionated web framework for Node.js used to build APIs and web servers with routing, middleware, and request handling.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is middleware in Express?","answer":"A function with access to req, res, and next, that runs during the request-response cycle — used for tasks like logging, authentication, or parsing data.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What does next() do in Express middleware?","answer":"Passes control to the next middleware function in the stack; without calling it, the request hangs.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between app.use() and app.get()?","answer":"app.use() applies middleware to all HTTP methods (and can be scoped to a path); app.get() only handles GET requests to a specific route.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"How do you handle errors in Express?","answer":"With error-handling middleware — a function with four parameters (err, req, res, next) placed after your routes.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is req.params used for?","answer":"Accessing dynamic segments of a URL, like the id in /users/:id.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is req.query used for?","answer":"Accessing query string parameters, like ?page=2&limit=10.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is req.body and what do you need to access it?","answer":"It holds data sent in the request body (like from a POST request); you need express.json() middleware to parse JSON bodies.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is express.json()?","answer":"Built-in middleware that parses incoming JSON request bodies and makes them available on req.body.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between PUT and PATCH?","answer":"PUT typically replaces an entire resource; PATCH updates only specific fields.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the purpose of a router in Express (express.Router())?","answer":"Lets you organize routes into separate modules/files instead of cramming everything into one file.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"How do you serve static files in Express?","answer":"Using express.static('foldername') middleware, e.g. app.use(express.static('public')).","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between res.send() and res.json()?","answer":"res.send() can send strings, objects, or buffers and Express infers content-type; res.json() explicitly sets content-type to application/json and stringifies the object.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is CORS and why does Express need to handle it?","answer":"Cross-Origin Resource Sharing — a browser security feature that blocks requests from a different origin unless the server explicitly allows it via headers.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"How would you apply middleware to only one specific route?","answer":"Pass it as an extra argument before the route handler, e.g. app.get('/profile', authMiddleware, handler).","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What HTTP status code would you return for a successful POST that creates a resource?","answer":"201 Created.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What status code means \"unauthorized\"?","answer":"401 — means the client isn't authenticated (missing/invalid credentials).","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between 401 and 403?","answer":"401 means you're not authenticated at all; 403 means you are authenticated but don't have permission to access that resource.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is the purpose of an async error wrapper in Express?","answer":"A helper function that wraps async route handlers to automatically catch errors and pass them to next(), avoiding repetitive try/catch blocks in every controller.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What does app.listen() do?","answer":"Starts the server and binds it to listen for requests on a given port.","topic":"Express.js","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is MongoDB?","answer":"A NoSQL, document-based database that stores data as flexible, JSON-like documents instead of rows and tables.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is a document in MongoDB?","answer":"A single record, stored in BSON format, similar to a JSON object.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is a collection?","answer":"A group of documents, roughly equivalent to a table in a relational database, but without enforced schema by default.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is BSON?","answer":"Binary JSON — the binary-encoded format MongoDB uses internally to store documents, more efficient and supports more data types than plain JSON.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is an ObjectId?","answer":"A unique 12-byte identifier MongoDB automatically generates for each document's _id field.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is Mongoose?","answer":"An ODM (Object Data Modeling) library for MongoDB and Node.js that adds schemas, validation, and convenient query methods on top of the raw MongoDB driver.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is a schema in Mongoose?","answer":"A blueprint that defines the structure, data types, and validation rules for documents in a collection.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between a Mongoose schema and a model?","answer":"A schema defines the structure; a model is the compiled version of that schema that you actually use to query and manipulate the database.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"How do you make a field required in Mongoose?","answer":"By setting { required: true } in the schema definition for that field.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What does unique: true do in a Mongoose schema?","answer":"Creates a unique index on that field, so MongoDB will reject documents with a duplicate value for it.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is the difference between find() and findOne()?","answer":"find() returns an array of all matching documents; findOne() returns only the first match, or null.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What does findByIdAndUpdate() do?","answer":"Finds a document by its _id and updates it in one call.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between save() and create() in Mongoose?","answer":"create() builds and saves a document in one step; save() is called on an already-instantiated document to persist changes.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is populate() used for?","answer":"Replacing a referenced ObjectId field with the actual document it points to, effectively simulating a JOIN across collections.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is an index in MongoDB and why does it matter?","answer":"A data structure that speeds up queries on a field; without one, MongoDB has to scan every document (a full collection scan), which gets slow at scale.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What does the aggregation pipeline do?","answer":"Processes documents through a series of stages (like $match, $group, $sort) to transform, filter, and analyze data.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What does $match do in an aggregation pipeline?","answer":"Filters documents, similar to a WHERE clause in SQL.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What does $group do?","answer":"Groups documents by a specified field and lets you compute aggregates like sum, average, or count.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is $lookup used for?","answer":"Performs a join-like operation between two collections within an aggregation pipeline.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between populate() and $lookup?","answer":"populate() is a Mongoose-specific convenience method for simple reference population; $lookup is a native MongoDB aggregation stage, more powerful and works even without Mongoose.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is a transaction in MongoDB?","answer":"A way to group multiple operations so they either all succeed or all fail together, ensuring data consistency across multiple documents/collections.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"When would you need a MongoDB transaction?","answer":"When multiple related writes must succeed together, like transferring money between two accounts — if one write fails, both should roll back.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between skip/limit and cursor-based pagination?","answer":"skip/limit re-scans and discards documents each time, getting slower on large offsets; cursor-based pagination uses a reference point (like the last document's _id) to jump directly to the next page, which stays fast at scale.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is a one-to-many relationship in MongoDB, and how is it usually modeled?","answer":"One document related to many others — often modeled by storing a reference (ObjectId) to the \"one\" side inside each of the \"many\" documents, then using populate() to join them.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between embedding and referencing in MongoDB schema design?","answer":"Embedding nests related data directly inside a document (fast reads, but can duplicate data); referencing stores an ObjectId pointing to another document (more normalized, but needs an extra query/populate to retrieve).","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is MongoDB Atlas?","answer":"MongoDB's official cloud-hosted database service.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What does mongoose.connect() do?","answer":"Establishes a connection between your Node app and a MongoDB database.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is schema validation in Mongoose used for?","answer":"Enforcing rules like required fields, data types, min/max length, or custom validators before data is saved.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's a virtual field in Mongoose?","answer":"A property that isn't stored in the database but is computed from other fields, e.g. combining firstName and lastName into fullName.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between SQL and NoSQL databases?","answer":"SQL databases (like PostgreSQL) use structured tables with fixed schemas and relationships enforced via foreign keys; NoSQL databases (like MongoDB) use flexible, often schema-less documents, better suited for rapidly changing or unstructured data.","topic":"MongoDB & Mongoose","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What does REST stand for?","answer":"Representational State Transfer — an architectural style for designing networked APIs around resources and standard HTTP methods.","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What are the main HTTP methods used in REST APIs?","answer":"GET (read), POST (create), PUT/PATCH (update), DELETE (remove).","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What makes an API \"RESTful\"?","answer":"It's stateless, resource-based (URLs represent resources, not actions), and uses standard HTTP methods and status codes consistently.","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What does \"stateless\" mean in REST?","answer":"Each request from a client must contain all the information needed to process it — the server doesn't store any session state between requests.","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's a good example of a RESTful URL structure?","answer":"/api/users/123/orders — resource-based, nested logically, no verbs in the path.","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"Why is /getUser?id=123 considered bad REST design?","answer":"It uses a verb in the URL instead of relying on the HTTP method (GET) to indicate the action; RESTful design would use /users/123.","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is idempotency in the context of REST APIs?","answer":"An operation is idempotent if calling it multiple times has the same effect as calling it once — GET, PUT, and DELETE are expected to be idempotent; POST is not.","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What status code should a successful GET request return?","answer":"200 OK.","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What status code means \"resource not found\"?","answer":"404.","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What status code indicates a server-side error?","answer":"500 Internal Server Error.","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is API versioning and why is it used?","answer":"Adding a version number to your API routes (e.g. /api/v1/) so you can change or improve the API without breaking existing clients still using the older version.","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between authentication and authorization in the context of an API?","answer":"Authentication verifies who the user is; authorization determines what that user is allowed to do.","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is rate limiting and why would an API need it?","answer":"Restricting how many requests a client can make in a given time window, to prevent abuse, brute-force attacks, or server overload.","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is pagination and why is it important in API design?","answer":"Splitting large result sets into smaller chunks (pages) so responses stay fast and don't overwhelm the client or server.","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is HATEOAS?","answer":"Hypermedia As The Engine Of Application State — a REST principle where API responses include links to related actions/resources, so clients can navigate the API dynamically instead of hardcoding URLs.","topic":"REST API Design","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is JWT?","answer":"JSON Web Token — a compact, signed token used to securely transmit user identity/claims between client and server.","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What are the three parts of a JWT?","answer":"Header, payload, and signature — separated by dots.","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"Is data inside a JWT encrypted?","answer":"No — it's encoded (Base64), not encrypted. Anyone can decode and read the payload; the signature only proves it hasn't been tampered with.","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is bcrypt used for?","answer":"Hashing passwords before storing them, so plain-text passwords are never saved in the database.","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"Why do you hash passwords instead of encrypting them?","answer":"Hashing is one-way — it can't be reversed to get the original password back, which is safer than encryption (which can be decrypted with a key).","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's a salt in password hashing?","answer":"Random data added to a password before hashing, so identical passwords don't produce identical hashes — protects against precomputed rainbow-table attacks.","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between storing a JWT in localStorage vs an httpOnly cookie?","answer":"localStorage is accessible via JavaScript, making it vulnerable to XSS attacks; httpOnly cookies can't be accessed by JavaScript at all, making them safer for storing tokens.","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is XSS?","answer":"Cross-Site Scripting — an attack where malicious scripts are injected into a webpage and executed in a user's browser, often to steal tokens or data.","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is CSRF?","answer":"Cross-Site Request Forgery — an attack that tricks a logged-in user's browser into making unwanted requests to a site they're authenticated on.","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"Why do access tokens usually have a short expiry?","answer":"To limit the damage if a token is stolen — a short-lived token becomes useless quickly, unlike a long-lived one.","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is a refresh token?","answer":"A long-lived token used to obtain a new access token without forcing the user to log in again.","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is refresh token rotation?","answer":"Issuing a new refresh token each time one is used, and invalidating the old one — reduces the risk if a refresh token is ever stolen.","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is role-based access control (RBAC)?","answer":"A system where users are assigned roles (like admin, user, moderator), and access to resources/actions is granted based on that role.","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What is OAuth?","answer":"An authorization framework that lets users grant a third-party app limited access to their account on another service (like logging in with Google) without sharing their password.","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"},
{"question":"What's the difference between authentication middleware and authorization middleware in Express?","answer":"Authentication middleware checks whether the user is logged in (valid token); authorization middleware checks whether that logged-in user has permission for the specific action/resource.","topic":"Authentication","difficulty":"beginner"}
