{"question":"What is a WebSocket?","answer":"A protocol that keeps a persistent, two-way connection open between client and server, allowing real-time data exchange without repeated HTTP requests.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"How is a WebSocket different from a regular HTTP request?","answer":"HTTP is request-response — the client asks, the server answers, then the connection closes. WebSockets stay open, so either side can send data at any time.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is HTTP polling and why is it inefficient compared to WebSockets?","answer":"Polling means the client repeatedly asks the server \"anything new?\" at intervals, wasting requests when there's nothing new; WebSockets push data only when something actually happens.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is Socket.io?","answer":"A library built on top of WebSockets (with fallbacks) that simplifies real-time, bidirectional communication between client and server.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's the difference between socket.emit() and io.emit()?","answer":"socket.emit() sends to one specific connected client; io.emit() sends to every connected client.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What does socket.broadcast.emit() do?","answer":"Sends an event to every connected client except the one that triggered it.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is a \"room\" in Socket.io?","answer":"A named group that sockets can join, letting you broadcast messages to a subset of connected clients instead of everyone.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"How does a socket join a room?","answer":"Using socket.join(roomName).","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's the difference between io.to(room).emit() and socket.to(room).emit()?","answer":"io.to(room).emit() sends to everyone in the room including the sender; socket.to(room).emit() sends to everyone in the room except the sender.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is a namespace in Socket.io?","answer":"A way to split communication into separate channels within a single server connection, e.g. /chat vs /admin, each with its own events and logic.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's the difference between a room and a namespace?","answer":"A namespace is a separate communication channel set up in your server code; a room is a dynamic grouping within a namespace that sockets join/leave at runtime.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"How would you detect when a user disconnects in Socket.io?","answer":"Listen for the built-in \"disconnect\" event on the socket.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why is it important to clean up on disconnect (e.g. clearInterval, removing from online list)?","answer":"To prevent memory leaks and stale data — without cleanup, timers keep running and disconnected users may still appear \"online.\"","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is socket.io-client used for?","answer":"The client-side library that connects a browser (or other client) to a Socket.io server.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"How would you implement a typing indicator in a chat app?","answer":"Emit a \"typing\" event when the user types, broadcast it to others in the room (excluding the sender), and emit \"stopTyping\" after a pause using a debounce/timeout.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"How would you scale a Socket.io app to handle thousands of concurrent users?","answer":"Use a Redis adapter to sync events across multiple server instances, since a single Node process/socket connection can't handle unlimited concurrent connections alone.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why can't you just load-balance Socket.io servers the same way as stateless REST APIs?","answer":"Because WebSocket connections are persistent and stateful — a client stays connected to one specific server instance, so you need \"sticky sessions\" or a shared adapter (like Redis) to keep them in sync.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is socket.io middleware (io.use())?","answer":"A function that runs before a socket connection is accepted, often used to verify auth tokens and reject unauthorized connections early.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What happens if a client's internet connection drops temporarily — does Socket.io reconnect automatically?","answer":"Yes, by default Socket.io attempts to automatically reconnect using exponential backoff.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is an acknowledgement callback in Socket.io?","answer":"A function passed as an extra argument to emit() that lets the receiving side send a response back, similar to a normal function return value.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"How would you store chat message history so it persists after a page refresh?","answer":"Save each message to a database (like MongoDB) when it's sent, and query recent messages for that room when a user joins/reconnects.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's a real-world use case for Socket.io rooms beyond chat apps?","answer":"Live sports score updates per game, collaborative document editing per document, or multiplayer game state per game session.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is the difference between socket.data and a global object like an \"onlineUsers\" tracker?","answer":"socket.data stores information scoped to that one connection (like username, room); a global tracker object stores info across all connections, needed for things like an online users list.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why might you choose Server-Sent Events (SSE) over WebSockets for some real-time features?","answer":"SSE is simpler and works over plain HTTP when you only need one-way server-to-client updates (like notifications), without needing full bidirectional communication.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is the purpose of io.sockets.sockets or io.fetchSockets()?","answer":"To get the actual list of currently connected sockets, useful for building accurate online-user lists instead of relying only on manually tracked state.","topic":"WebSockets & Socket.io","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why not just use one long-lived token instead of access + refresh tokens?","answer":"A single long-lived token is riskier if stolen — it stays valid for a long time. Splitting into a short-lived access token and a longer-lived refresh token limits exposure while still avoiding constant re-logins.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Where should a refresh token be stored on the client, and why?","answer":"In an httpOnly cookie — keeps it inaccessible to JavaScript, reducing the risk of theft via XSS.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What should happen if a stolen refresh token is used?","answer":"With rotation in place, using an old (already-rotated) refresh token can be detected and used as a signal to revoke all tokens for that user, forcing re-authentication.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is 2FA (Two-Factor Authentication)?","answer":"An extra layer of security requiring a second proof of identity beyond a password, like an OTP sent via email/SMS or an authenticator app code.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"How do you generate a secure OTP in Node.js?","answer":"Typically using a cryptographically random number generator (like crypto.randomInt()) rather than Math.random(), which isn't secure for this purpose.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why should an OTP expire after a short time?","answer":"To limit the window an attacker has to guess or intercept it and use it before it becomes invalid.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's a common way to store and check OTP expiry in MongoDB?","answer":"Save the OTP with an expiresAt timestamp field, and check if the current time is past that value when verifying.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is OAuth 2.0's \"authorization code\" flow, at a high level?","answer":"The user is redirected to the provider (e.g. Google) to log in and approve access; the provider redirects back with a code, which your server exchanges for an access token.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is Passport.js?","answer":"A popular Node.js authentication middleware that supports many strategies, including OAuth providers like Google, Facebook, and GitHub.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is a \"callback URL\" in OAuth?","answer":"The URL the provider redirects the user back to after they approve (or deny) access, where your server handles the response.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What information does Google typically return after successful OAuth login?","answer":"Basic profile info like name, email, and a unique Google account ID — used to find or create a matching user in your database.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's the \"find or create\" pattern in OAuth login flows?","answer":"Checking if a user with that provider's ID/email already exists in your database; if yes, log them in, if no, create a new user record.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's the difference between authentication and session management?","answer":"Authentication verifies identity at login; session management is how the server keeps track of that authenticated state across subsequent requests (via tokens or cookies).","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why is role checking usually done in middleware rather than inside each controller?","answer":"It centralizes the logic, avoids repeating the same check in every route, and keeps authorization consistent across the app.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's the risk of storing user roles only in the JWT payload without ever re-checking the database?","answer":"If a role is changed or revoked, the old token still carries the old role until it expires — role changes may not take effect until the token refreshes.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is account lockout, and why is it used?","answer":"Temporarily blocking login attempts after several failed tries, to slow down brute-force password guessing attacks.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"How does rate limiting help protect a login route specifically?","answer":"It limits how many login attempts can come from one IP in a given time window, making brute-force attacks impractical.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's the difference between session-based auth and token-based (JWT) auth?","answer":"Session-based auth stores session state on the server (often in a store like Redis) and gives the client a session ID; token-based auth is stateless — the server verifies the token itself without needing to look anything up.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why might session-based auth be harder to scale across multiple servers than JWT?","answer":"Because the session data needs to be shared/accessible across all server instances (e.g. via a shared store like Redis), whereas a JWT is self-contained and any server can verify it independently.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is the principle of least privilege, in the context of user roles?","answer":"Users and systems should only have the minimum level of access necessary to do their job — not more.","topic":"Advanced Authentication","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is Multer?","answer":"An Express middleware for handling multipart/form-data, primarily used for file uploads.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's the difference between disk storage and memory storage in Multer?","answer":"Disk storage saves the uploaded file directly to the server's filesystem; memory storage keeps it as a buffer in memory, useful when you're about to forward it elsewhere (like Cloudinary) without needing to save it locally first.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why might you prefer memory storage when uploading to Cloudinary?","answer":"You can upload the buffer directly to Cloudinary without an unnecessary extra step of writing to and then deleting from local disk.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is Cloudinary used for?","answer":"A cloud service for storing, transforming, and serving images/videos, so you don't have to manage file storage on your own server.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why avoid storing uploaded files directly on your own server in production?","answer":"Servers are often ephemeral (especially on platforms like Render/Railway) — files can be wiped on redeploy, and it doesn't scale well across multiple server instances.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What do you typically store in MongoDB after uploading a file to Cloudinary?","answer":"Just the returned URL (and maybe a public ID for later deletion), not the file itself.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"How would you delete a previously uploaded file when a user updates their profile picture?","answer":"Call Cloudinary's delete/destroy API using the stored public ID before or after saving the new upload.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is Nodemailer?","answer":"A Node.js library used to send emails from a server, supporting SMTP and various providers like Gmail or Brevo.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is SMTP?","answer":"Simple Mail Transfer Protocol — the standard protocol used to send emails between servers.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why can't you just send emails directly without an SMTP provider?","answer":"Email providers require authentication and have spam/reputation systems; using a proper SMTP service ensures reliable delivery and avoids being flagged as spam.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's the benefit of using HTML email templates instead of plain text?","answer":"They allow for branded, styled, more engaging emails (buttons, logos, formatting) rather than plain unstyled text.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"How would you inject dynamic content (like a user's name or OTP) into an email template?","answer":"Use string interpolation or a templating approach to insert variables into the HTML before sending.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's a common use case for sending a \"welcome email\" on registration?","answer":"Confirming successful signup and setting expectations, sometimes including account verification steps.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's the difference between multipart/form-data and application/json in the context of uploads?","answer":"multipart/form-data is required for sending files (and can include other fields); application/json can't carry binary file data directly.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why might you limit accepted file types and file size when handling uploads?","answer":"To prevent abuse (huge uploads overwhelming storage/bandwidth) and reduce security risk from unexpected file types.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What HTTP status would you typically return if a file upload exceeds the allowed size limit?","answer":"413 Payload Too Large.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's a password reset email flow typically look like?","answer":"User requests reset → server generates a unique, expiring token → emails a link containing that token → user clicks it and submits a new password → server verifies the token before updating the password.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why should a password reset token be single-use and short-lived?","answer":"To limit the window in which the link could be intercepted or reused maliciously.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's the risk of exposing whether an email exists in your system during a \"forgot password\" flow?","answer":"It can leak information to attackers about which emails are registered users, useful for targeted attacks — many systems respond the same way whether the email exists or not.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Where should Cloudinary/SMTP credentials be stored in your project?","answer":"In environment variables (.env), never hardcoded or committed to version control.","topic":"File Uploads & Email","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is the MVC pattern?","answer":"Model-View-Controller — an architecture that separates data logic (Model), request/response handling (Controller), and presentation (View, often not used in pure APIs).","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"In an Express API without views, what replaces the \"View\" part of MVC?","answer":"The JSON response itself — the API returns data instead of rendering HTML.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What belongs in a controller vs a service layer?","answer":"A controller handles the request/response cycle (parsing input, sending output); a service holds the actual business logic, keeping controllers thin.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why separate business logic into a service layer instead of putting it all in the controller?","answer":"It's more reusable (services can be called from multiple places), easier to test independently, and keeps controllers focused on HTTP concerns only.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is a custom error class (like AppError) used for?","answer":"Standardizing errors with a message and status code so they can be handled consistently by a global error handler.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is a global error handler in Express?","answer":"A special middleware (with four parameters: err, req, res, next) placed at the end of the middleware stack that catches all errors thrown anywhere in the app.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why use an async error wrapper function around route handlers?","answer":"To automatically catch errors from async functions and forward them to next(), avoiding repetitive try/catch blocks in every single route.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What does express-rate-limit do?","answer":"Limits how many requests a client can make within a set time window, helping prevent abuse and brute-force attacks.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is Helmet.js?","answer":"Middleware that sets various secure HTTP headers automatically to protect against common web vulnerabilities.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What does mongo-sanitize protect against?","answer":"NoSQL injection attacks, where malicious MongoDB query operators are passed in as user input.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is NoSQL injection?","answer":"An attack where a malicious user submits MongoDB query operators (like $gt or $ne) as input, potentially bypassing intended logic such as authentication checks.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why version an API (e.g. /api/v1/)?","answer":"So you can introduce breaking changes in a new version without disrupting clients still relying on the older one.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's the risk of pushing a .env file to GitHub?","answer":"It exposes secrets like API keys, database credentials, and JWT secrets publicly, which can be exploited by anyone who finds the repo.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is dotenv used for?","answer":"Loading environment variables from a .env file into process.env during development.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why have separate .env files for development and production?","answer":"Different environments often need different values (e.g. different database URLs or API keys), and you don't want dev settings accidentally used in production or vice versa.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is a config folder pattern used for?","answer":"Centralizing access to environment variables and settings in one place, instead of scattering process.env references throughout the codebase.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why is \"separation of concerns\" considered good practice in backend architecture?","answer":"It makes code easier to read, test, and maintain — each part of the system has one clear responsibility instead of mixed, tangled logic.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What does \"DRY\" mean in coding practice?","answer":"Don't Repeat Yourself — avoid duplicating logic; extract shared code into reusable functions/modules.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why might you avoid deeply nested if/else or callback structures?","answer":"They're harder to read, debug, and maintain — often better handled with early returns, async/await, or breaking logic into smaller functions.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's the benefit of consistent error response formatting across an API (e.g. always { success, message, data })?","answer":"It makes the API predictable for frontend developers, since every error/success response follows the same shape regardless of which endpoint was called.","topic":"API Architecture","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is a unit test?","answer":"A test that checks a single function or small piece of logic in isolation, without dependencies like a database or network.","topic":"Testing","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is an integration test?","answer":"A test that checks how multiple parts of the system work together, like hitting an actual API endpoint and verifying the full request/response cycle.","topic":"Testing","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is Jest?","answer":"A JavaScript testing framework used for writing and running unit and integration tests, including assertions and mocking.","topic":"Testing","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is Supertest used for?","answer":"Making HTTP requests to your Express app in tests, without needing to actually start a running server on a port.","topic":"Testing","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What does \"mocking\" mean in testing?","answer":"Replacing a real dependency (like a database call or external API) with a fake version that returns controlled, predictable data, so tests don't rely on external systems.","topic":"Testing","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why mock a database in tests instead of using the real one?","answer":"Tests run faster, don't risk corrupting real data, and stay reliable even if the actual database is down or slow.","topic":"Testing","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What is Test-Driven Development (TDD)?","answer":"A development approach where you write a failing test first, then write just enough code to make it pass, then refactor.","topic":"Testing","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What's a good first test to write for a login endpoint?","answer":"Testing that valid credentials return a 200 with a token, and invalid credentials return a 401 with an appropriate error message.","topic":"Testing","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"Why is it useful to test both the \"happy path\" and error cases?","answer":"The happy path confirms the feature works as intended; error cases confirm the app fails safely and predictably instead of crashing or leaking information.","topic":"Testing","difficulty":"intermediate"},
{"question":"What does \"test coverage\" mean?","answer":"A metric showing what percentage of your code is actually executed by your test suite — high coverage doesn't guarantee correctness, but low coverage means large blind spots.","topic":"Testing","difficulty":"intermediate"}
