{"question":"What is Docker?","answer":"A platform that packages an application and its dependencies into a container — a lightweight, isolated environment that runs the same way regardless of the underlying machine.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is a container?","answer":"A running instance of a Docker image — an isolated process with its own filesystem, but sharing the host machine's OS kernel, making it much lighter than a full virtual machine.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What's the difference between a Docker image and a Docker container?","answer":"An image is a static, read-only blueprint (like a class); a container is a running instance of that image (like an object created from the class).","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is a Dockerfile?","answer":"A text file containing instructions for building a Docker image — what base image to use, what to copy in, what commands to run, and what to start when the container runs.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What does the FROM instruction do in a Dockerfile?","answer":"Specifies the base image to build on top of, e.g. FROM node:20.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What's the difference between COPY and RUN in a Dockerfile?","answer":"COPY moves files from your local machine into the image; RUN executes a command during the image build process, like installing dependencies.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What does CMD do in a Dockerfile, and how is it different from RUN?","answer":"CMD specifies the default command to run when a container starts; RUN executes during the image build itself, not when the container runs.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is Docker Compose?","answer":"A tool for defining and running multi-container applications using a single YAML file, so you can spin up your app, database, and other services together with one command.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"Why would you use Docker Compose instead of running multiple docker run commands manually?","answer":"It's easier to manage — one file defines all services, networks, and volumes, and one command (docker-compose up) starts everything together correctly configured.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is a Docker volume?","answer":"A mechanism for persisting data outside a container's filesystem, so data isn't lost when the container is removed or restarted.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"Why would you need a volume for a database running in Docker?","answer":"Without one, all the database's data would be wiped every time the container is removed or rebuilt.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is a Docker network used for?","answer":"Letting containers communicate with each other (e.g. your app container talking to your database container) using container names instead of IP addresses.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What does 'containerizing' an app actually solve?","answer":"The 'it works on my machine' problem — the app runs in the exact same environment (OS, dependencies, versions) everywhere, from a developer's laptop to production.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is a .dockerignore file for?","answer":"Excludes files/folders (like node_modules or .env) from being copied into the Docker image, keeping it smaller and avoiding leaking secrets.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is CI/CD?","answer":"Continuous Integration/Continuous Deployment — automating the process of testing and deploying code whenever changes are pushed, instead of doing it manually.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is GitHub Actions?","answer":"A CI/CD tool built into GitHub that lets you automate workflows, like running tests or deploying an app, triggered by events such as a push or pull request.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What's a typical CI/CD pipeline step order for a Node app?","answer":"Install dependencies → run tests → build (if needed) → deploy, usually stopping the pipeline early if any step fails.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is SSH used for in a DevOps context?","answer":"Securely connecting to and controlling a remote server's command line, often used to manually manage or deploy to a VPS.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is a reverse proxy?","answer":"A server that sits in front of your application and forwards client requests to it, often used for load balancing, SSL termination, or serving multiple apps on one domain.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is Nginx commonly used for in a full-stack deployment?","answer":"Acting as a reverse proxy to route traffic — e.g. serving a React frontend and forwarding /api requests to a separate backend, all on one domain.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is a VPS?","answer":"Virtual Private Server — a virtual machine rented from a provider (like DigitalOcean or Hetzner) that gives you full control over the server environment, unlike managed platforms.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What's the difference between deploying to a managed platform (like Render) vs a VPS?","answer":"Managed platforms handle server setup/maintenance for you with less control; a VPS gives you full control but you're responsible for setup, security, and maintenance yourself.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What are file permissions in Linux, broadly?","answer":"Settings that control who can read, write, or execute a file — owner, group, and everyone else each have their own set of permissions.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What does the Node cluster module let you do?","answer":"Spawn multiple worker processes that share the same server port, letting a Node app use all available CPU cores instead of just one.","topic":"Docker & DevOps","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is Redis?","answer":"An in-memory data store often used for caching, session storage, pub/sub messaging, and rate limiting, valued for its speed since it keeps data in RAM.","topic":"Redis","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"Why is Redis so much faster than a traditional database for certain tasks?","answer":"It stores data in memory (RAM) rather than on disk, so reads and writes are extremely fast, though data is more volatile unless persistence is configured.","topic":"Redis","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is caching, and why does it matter?","answer":"Storing the result of an expensive operation (like a slow database query) temporarily, so repeated requests can be served instantly instead of recomputing it every time.","topic":"Redis","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What's a good real-world example of when to cache data with Redis?","answer":"A product listing page that doesn't change often — cache the result so thousands of users hitting it don't all trigger a fresh, expensive database query.","topic":"Redis","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is cache invalidation, and why is it considered a hard problem?","answer":"Knowing when cached data is outdated and needs to be refreshed or removed — get it wrong and users either see stale data or you lose the caching benefit entirely.","topic":"Redis","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"How can Redis be used for session storage?","answer":"Instead of storing session data in server memory (which doesn't scale across multiple servers), sessions are stored in Redis, which all server instances can access.","topic":"Redis","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is pub/sub, and how does Redis support it?","answer":"A messaging pattern where publishers send messages to a channel and subscribers listening on that channel receive them; Redis has built-in pub/sub commands for this.","topic":"Redis","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"How could Redis help scale a Socket.io app across multiple servers?","answer":"Using the Redis adapter, events emitted on one server instance get broadcast through Redis to all other instances, so users connected to different servers still receive the same real-time events.","topic":"Redis","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"How can Redis be used for rate limiting?","answer":"Storing a counter per user/IP with an expiry time, incrementing it on each request, and rejecting requests once the counter exceeds the allowed limit within that time window.","topic":"Redis","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is TTL in the context of Redis?","answer":"Time To Live — how long a key stays in Redis before it automatically expires and gets removed, commonly used for caching and OTPs.","topic":"Redis","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is horizontal scaling?","answer":"Adding more servers/machines to handle increased load, as opposed to making one server more powerful.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is vertical scaling?","answer":"Increasing the resources (CPU, RAM) of a single existing server, rather than adding more servers.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is load balancing?","answer":"Distributing incoming requests across multiple servers so no single server becomes overwhelmed.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is database sharding?","answer":"Splitting a large database into smaller pieces (shards) spread across multiple servers, so no single machine has to store or process all the data.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is database replication?","answer":"Keeping copies of the same database on multiple servers, improving read performance and providing redundancy if one server fails.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What's the difference between sharding and replication?","answer":"Sharding splits different data across servers; replication duplicates the same data across servers.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is the CAP theorem?","answer":"A principle stating a distributed system can only guarantee two out of three: Consistency, Availability, and Partition tolerance, not all three at once.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What does 'consistency' mean in the CAP theorem?","answer":"Every read receives the most recent write or an error — all nodes see the same data at the same time.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What does 'availability' mean in the CAP theorem?","answer":"Every request receives a response, even if it's not the most up-to-date data.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What does 'partition tolerance' mean in the CAP theorem?","answer":"The system continues to function even if network communication between nodes breaks down.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"How would you design a basic URL shortener at a high level?","answer":"Generate a unique short code for each submitted URL, store the mapping (short code → original URL) in a database, and redirect users who visit the short URL to the original one.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What's a key design decision when building a URL shortener at scale?","answer":"How to generate unique short codes efficiently without collisions — common approaches include base62 encoding of an auto-incrementing ID, or hashing with collision checks.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What does 'fan-out on write' mean in the context of a social media feed?","answer":"When a user posts, the post is immediately pushed/copied into all their followers' feeds at write-time, making reads fast but writes potentially expensive for users with huge followings.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What does 'fan-out on read' mean?","answer":"Instead of pre-computing feeds, the system gathers posts from all followed users at the moment the feed is requested — writes are cheap, but reads can be slower.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"Why might a platform like Twitter use a hybrid of fan-out on write and fan-out on read?","answer":"Fan-out on write works well for regular users, but for celebrities with millions of followers, fan-out on write at post-time would be too expensive — so their posts are fetched on read instead.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is a microservices architecture?","answer":"Building an application as a collection of small, independent services, each responsible for a specific piece of functionality, communicating over a network.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is a monolith?","answer":"An application built as a single, unified codebase and deployable unit, where all functionality lives together rather than being split into separate services.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What's a downside of microservices compared to a monolith?","answer":"Increased complexity — network calls between services, harder debugging across services, and more infrastructure to manage.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"When might a monolith be the better choice over microservices?","answer":"For smaller teams or early-stage products, where the added complexity of microservices isn't justified yet by the scale or team size.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is a message queue, and why is it useful?","answer":"A system that lets different parts of an application communicate asynchronously by sending messages to a queue, which are processed later — useful for decoupling and handling background jobs.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What's an example of when you'd use a message queue like RabbitMQ or Bull?","answer":"Sending a large batch of emails — instead of making the user wait while all emails send synchronously, you queue the job and process it in the background.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is a CDN, and why is it used?","answer":"Content Delivery Network — a network of servers distributed globally that cache and serve static assets (images, CSS, JS) from a location physically closer to the user, reducing load time.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is database query optimization, broadly?","answer":"Improving how efficiently a query runs, often through indexing, restructuring the query, or analyzing an execution/explain plan to find bottlenecks.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is an 'explain plan' used for?","answer":"Showing how a database will actually execute a given query, helping identify whether it's using indexes efficiently or doing a slow full scan.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is profiling, in the context of application performance?","answer":"Measuring where time and resources are actually being spent in your code, to identify bottlenecks instead of guessing.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"Why is 'start reading about system design casually' often recommended, even before you have a system design interview?","answer":"System design thinking (tradeoffs, scale, architecture) takes time to build intuition for, and is what separates mid-level from senior-level engineering judgment.","topic":"System Design","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is the OWASP Top 10?","answer":"A regularly updated list of the most critical and common web application security risks, maintained by the Open Web Application Security Project.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is SQL injection?","answer":"An attack where malicious SQL code is inserted into an input field and executed by the database, potentially exposing or destroying data.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"How do parameterized queries help prevent SQL injection?","answer":"They separate the SQL code from the user-supplied data, so input is always treated as data, never as executable code.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is CSRF protection, and how is it commonly implemented?","answer":"Preventing Cross-Site Request Forgery, often using anti-CSRF tokens that must be included in state-changing requests, which an attacker's malicious site wouldn't have access to.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is penetration testing?","answer":"Simulating real-world attacks against a system to find security vulnerabilities before actual attackers do.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is a security header, and why does it matter?","answer":"An HTTP response header that instructs the browser to enforce certain security behaviors, like preventing your site from being embedded in an iframe (clickjacking protection) or restricting where scripts can load from.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What does the Content-Security-Policy header do?","answer":"Restricts which sources (scripts, styles, images) a browser is allowed to load content from, reducing the risk of XSS attacks.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is the principle behind 'never trust user input'?","answer":"Any data coming from the client (form fields, query params, headers) could be manipulated, so it must always be validated and sanitized on the server before use.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What's the difference between validation and sanitization?","answer":"Validation checks whether input meets expected rules (e.g. is it a valid email) and rejects it if not; sanitization actively cleans/modifies input to remove or neutralize potentially harmful content.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"Why is it risky to return detailed error messages (like stack traces) to the client in production?","answer":"It can leak information about your server's internals, file structure, or dependencies, which an attacker could use to find vulnerabilities.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is a brute-force attack?","answer":"Repeatedly trying many password/credential combinations until one works, typically mitigated with rate limiting and account lockouts.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is clickjacking?","answer":"An attack where a malicious site tricks a user into clicking something different from what they perceive, often by overlaying invisible elements on top of a legitimate page.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What does the X-Frame-Options header protect against?","answer":"Prevents your site from being embedded inside an iframe on another site, which helps protect against clickjacking.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"Why is HTTPS important beyond just encryption?","answer":"It also verifies the authenticity of the server you're communicating with (via SSL certificates), preventing man-in-the-middle attacks in addition to encrypting data in transit.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is a man-in-the-middle attack?","answer":"An attack where a third party secretly intercepts and potentially alters communication between two parties who believe they're communicating directly.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"Why should sensitive data (like passwords) never be logged?","answer":"Logs are often stored, shared, or reviewed by multiple people/systems, and could be a security leak if they contain plaintext sensitive information.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is dependency vulnerability scanning?","answer":"Checking your project's installed packages against known security vulnerability databases, often via tools like npm audit, to catch outdated or unsafe dependencies.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"},
{"question":"What is the purpose of running npm audit?","answer":"Scans your installed dependencies for known security vulnerabilities and suggests fixes or updates.","topic":"Advanced Security","difficulty":"Advanced"}
